nd 1 of a 20-hand match. We both started with 20,000 chips. Right now, I\'m at 19,900.  The blinds are likely 50/100, given the history, but I need to be sure about that.  My opponent also has 19,900.  Pre-flop, we both just checked. The flop is Jack of Spades, 9 of Spades, Queen of Hearts. My hand is Jack of Diamonds, 10 of Spades.\n\nAlright, analyzing the board and my hand... I have top pair, which is nice, but the kicker is only a 10. That makes it potentially vulnerable.  More excitingly, I have an open-ended straight draw!  I need either an 8 or a King.  And, I also have a backdoor flush draw with one spade in my hand and two on the board.\n\nMy opponent\'s actions are key. Pre-flop and on the flop, they checked. That\'s a passive play, maybe weak, or maybe they\'re slow-playing something. The board is wet with those spades; straights and flushes are possible. My top pair is threatened by overcards (King, Ace) or the completion of a straight or flush. However, I have that straight draw myself.  This board texture gives me a bunch of options to consider.\n\nI have a few strategic options: Check, Bet, Fold, or All-in. Fold is out of the question; this hand is too strong. All-in is too risky at this stage. That leaves me with check or bet. Checking is safe. I can control the pot size and still have good showdown value and a strong draw. But it also gives a free card to my opponent, which could be dangerous, particularly if they\'re on a flush draw with one spade on the board. Betting here is my other option. If I bet, I can try and build the pot for when I potentially hit my straight or win with top pair.  I want to charge draws and protect my hand. If I bet, and my opponent has a Jack or a nine, I\'m probably ahead and want to build the pot.  If they have a Queen, I\'m behind.  Betting seems like a natural move, given that the opponent checked to me. It\'s a way to define the hand and build value when I hit a straight or continue to be ahead.  A bet also lets me